insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-deck-is', 1, 'A deck is described as an input file rather than a model. What is the distinction?', '["A deck is the text form of the model, and the model is the binary form the simulator writes out after parsing it.", "A deck DESCRIBES a reservoir; the model is what the simulator builds when it reads the deck and starts solving.", "A deck is the static description and the model adds the wells and the schedule.", "There is no real distinction, since a simulator reconstructs the deck exactly from the model it builds."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The deck is upstream of everything: the pressure field, the saturations and the mass balances all live in the model the simulator constructs. That is why a deck a simulator ACCEPTS is not a deck that is right, since acceptance means the grammar parsed and the required blocks were present. Wells and the schedule are part of the deck, and a model cannot be inverted back into one.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-deck-is', 2, 'The Ekene deck is 940 lines of plain text. What follows about auditing a simulation study?', '["The deck is far too long to read line by line, so auditing has to rely on the results file instead, which is where the study''s conclusions actually live.", "The deck is generated, so auditing means checking the generator rather than its output.", "The deck is machine-readable but not human-readable, so auditing needs a specialised viewer.", "The deck is the least opaque part of the exercise, so a reader who can read one can audit the study."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Simulation has a reputation for opacity and the opacity is real, but it lives in the SOLVER rather than in the deck. Every line of the deck is a decision somebody made, written in ASCII. A generator encodes choices rather than removing them, and the deck is where those choices become visible again, which is why it is worth reading even when you did not write it.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-deck-is', 3, 'Where did the numbers in the Ekene deck come from?', '["From the four earlier courses of the reservoir module: the layer column, the rock curves, the oil properties and the production history each came from one of them.", "From the geoscience courses alone, since a deck is a static description of the reservoir.", "From a single PVT and core report supplied by the operator.", "From the simulator''s own defaults, adjusted until the model reproduced the field''s production."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The layer thicknesses are the waterflood course''s layer column, the rock curves the SCAL course''s Corey design, the oil the material balance course''s tank fluid, and the history the waterflood course''s monthly ledger. That is the point of the exercise: a deck is where all of it has to agree, and the places it fails to agree are the interesting part.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-deck-is', 4, 'The six deck sections must appear in a fixed order. Why is that a rule rather than a convention?', '["Because the file format stores a byte offset for each of the six sections in the header at the top of the deck, and reordering them would invalidate every offset.", "Because the sections are sorted alphabetically, and RUNSPEC through SCHEDULE happens to be that order.", "Because a simulator reads a deck in a single pass, so each section depends on the ones before it having been read.", "Because a simulator reads the deck twice and the second pass assumes the first pass found the sections in order."]'::jsonb, 2, 'RUNSPEC declares dimensions so GRID can be checked, GRID establishes cells so PROPS has something to apply to, PROPS defines fluids so SOLUTION can equilibrate, and SOLUTION sets the state SCHEDULE starts from. Nothing goes back to reinterpret an earlier section in the light of a later one. The order is not alphabetical and there is no header index.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-deck-is', 5, 'In the Ekene deck the GRID section runs from line 32 to line 168 and SCHEDULE from 311 to the end. What does that proportion tell you?', '["The grid is unusually large for a model of this size, which suggests per-cell property data.", "Two thirds of the deck is the well history, which is what a history-matched study looks like.", "The PROPS section has been left mostly at defaults, which is why the deck is dominated by SCHEDULE.", "The deck was generated rather than written, since a hand-written deck would balance the sections."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A deck that is nearly all SCHEDULE carries a long production history; one that is nearly all GRID is a big static model with a short run. Ekene''s GRID section is only 137 lines precisely BECAUSE most of its properties are constant and compress, and the PROPS section is a full set of tables rather than defaults.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-deck-is', 6, 'What does the SUMMARY section change about a simulation run?', '["Nothing about the physics; it is a request for output and the run is identical without it.", "It selects which cells the solver reports on, which changes the timestep the solver takes.", "It controls the report step interval, so a longer list produces finer output in time.", "It defines the vectors the solver computes, so omitting one saves the work of computing it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two runs of the same deck differing only in SUMMARY produce identical reservoir behaviour. That is worth being certain about, because it means vectors can be added freely on a re-run without perturbing the comparison. The practical cost of omitting one is that the quantity is unavailable afterwards and the only remedy is a re-run.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-deck-is', 7, 'The Ekene deck is in FIELD units and states so once, in RUNSPEC. What does that mean for a depth of 5055.774278215223 read from the GRID section?', '["It is metres, since depths are conventionally reported in metric units even inside a field-unit deck, which is why the mapped contact reads 1560.", "It is ambiguous without checking the TOPS block''s own unit declaration.", "It is feet, and a reader assuming metres would conclude the field is over three times deeper than it is.", "It is feet, but the difference is immaterial because the model is internally consistent either way."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing in the deck restates the units after RUNSPEC, so every number is in the deck''s units. Ekene was mapped in metres and its contact is at 1560 m, which the deck carries as 5118.110236220472 ft. The difference is very material: the depth decides the contact, the equilibration and the pressures.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-deck-is', 8, 'In a GRID block the token 4500*328.0839895013123 appears. What does the star mean?', '["Multiply: the product of the count and the value, which is the total grid extent.", "A wildcard: apply the value to all cells not otherwise assigned.", "A continuation marker: the value continues onto the following line.", "Repeat: four thousand five hundred copies of that value."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is a repeat count, and it is why a grid of 4500 cells needs eleven characters instead of 4500 numbers. Reading it as multiplication gives a cell size of about 1.5 million feet, which is absurd enough to catch; the same mistake on a smaller repeat count is not absurd and passes unnoticed.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-deck-is', 9, 'A keyword block is closed by a slash, and a record-per-line block needs one slash per record plus a final bare slash. What is the symptom of a forgotten closing slash?', '["The parser stops at the exact line carrying the missing slash and names that line in its error message, so the fix is always at the line reported.", "The reported error points at a line AFTER the real mistake, because the parser keeps reading the next keyword as another record.", "The block is silently truncated at the last complete record and the run continues.", "The whole section is skipped and the simulator falls back to defaults for it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That displacement is the single most recognisable deck syntax error. The same symptom comes from a wrong repeat count, where a block ends one value short and the parser consumes the next keyword. When a deck fails to parse in a block, count the values it should contain before looking at anything else.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-deck-is', 10, 'A field written as 1* in a record takes its default. Why is that described as dangerous?', '["Because a defaulted field is invisible: you cannot tell whether a value was considered and left at its default or never considered at all.", "Because the defaults differ between simulators, so a deck stops being portable the moment any field is defaulted and the same file gives different answers in different tools.", "Because the default is always zero, which is rarely what the author intended.", "Because a defaulted field raises an error in strict mode, so decks that use them cannot be validated."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The habit that helps is to write the values you care about explicitly even when they equal the default, so a reader can see you meant them. Defaults are not universally zero, and they are a normal part of the format rather than an error condition.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-deck-is', 11, 'This course does not run a deck. What is the stated reason?', '["Running a deck requires a licence that the course cannot provide to learners.", "The Ekene deck is too large to run within a lesson''s time budget.", "Building a correct, complete and defensible deck is a distinct skill from operating a solver, and it is the one that decides whether the solver''s answer means anything.", "Simulation results are proprietary to the operator, so they cannot be published in course material."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The boundary is deliberate. Petrolord''s Reservoir Simulation Studio is where a Petrolord deck goes to be run, and what this course teaches is what to hand it. The Expert tier does spend a module reading results, and grades none of it, because the engines this course rests on emit decks and do geometry rather than solving flow equations.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-deck-is', 12, 'The course also declines to re-derive the physics behind the deck''s tables. What does it do instead when those answers disagree with each other?', '["It adopts whichever answer the simulator accepts and notes the other in passing.", "It re-opens the argument, since a deck cannot carry two inconsistent descriptions.", "It averages the two, which is the standard treatment for two defensible sources.", "It says so loudly, because a deck is exactly where such disagreements surface."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The relative permeability came from the SCAL course, the oil from Material Balance and the layer column from Waterflood Management, and this course puts their answers in a file. Where they disagree, with each other or with the volumetric booking, the course names the disagreement. What it does not do is re-litigate why the Corey exponent is 2.5.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-deck-is', 13, 'What does this course say about gridding?', '["It explains where the Ekene grid came from because you cannot audit a deck without knowing that, and it leaves geological modelling to a different discipline.", "It teaches gridding in full, since the grid is the largest section of a deck.", "It avoids the subject entirely, since the grid arrives from the geoscience workflow.", "It teaches corner-point geometry, faults and local refinement, which are what a real deck needs."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Professional tier explains that the deck''s structure came from kriging six mapped well tops and what that costs. Corner-point geometry, faults and local grid refinement are named as a different discipline with its own tools. This deck is a Cartesian box with a mapped top surface, which is the simplest structure that is still honest.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-deck-is', 14, 'A colleague says the deck was generated by software, so there is no need to read it. What is the answer?', '["Generated decks are validated on output before they are written, so reading one adds nothing at all that the validator has not already checked on your behalf.", "Generated decks are correct by construction, so reading is only worthwhile for hand-written ones.", "A generator encodes a series of choices rather than removing them, and the deck is where those choices become visible again.", "Generated decks cannot be read reliably, because generators emit machine-oriented formatting."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Something generated it, and that something encoded which convention, which contact, which fluid and which averaging. A validator checks that a deck can be built and read, not that it describes the field. Generated decks are perfectly readable and reading one is how the encoded choices are recovered.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-deck-is', 15, 'Which of these is the course''s own summary of what each tier does?', '["Associate builds a deck, Professional runs it against the field data, and Expert history matches it to the production record and forecasts from it.", "Associate reads a deck, Professional says where each number came from and whether the deck adds up, Expert builds one and proves it.", "Associate covers the grid, Professional the fluids, Expert the schedule.", "Associate learns the format, Professional learns the simulator, Expert learns the solver."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The three tiers are three kinds of reading and writing rather than three parts of the file. None of them runs a simulation, and the whole of what makes a study trustworthy is available anyway, which is the argument the course makes for its own scope.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 1, 'The Ekene grid is 30 by 30 by 5. How many cells is that, and how many columns?', '["4500 cells and 4500 columns, since every cell is the top of its own column in a block-centred grid.", "900 cells and 900 columns, because the five layers share one set of cell definitions between them.", "4500 cells and 900 columns.", "4500 cells and 150 columns, since a column is counted per layer rather than per areal position."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A column is an areal position, so there are nx times ny of them, and a cell is a column times a layer. The distinction matters immediately: TOPS carries one value per column and PORO one per cell, so the two blocks differ in length by a factor of the layer count.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 2, 'What does block-centred geometry mean for a single cell?', '["Everything about it is stated at its centre: one pressure, one saturation, one porosity, one permeability, with no variation inside it.", "Its eight corners are given explicitly, so it can be an arbitrary hexahedron rather than a box.", "Its properties are averaged over its faces rather than at its middle.", "Its position is stated as an explicit coordinate rather than derived from the cell sizes of its neighbours, which is what corner-point geometry does."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A cell is the smallest thing the model can distinguish, and its position follows from the cell sizes rather than from a stated coordinate. Explicit corners are corner-point geometry, which is the more general form this deck does not need because its layers are continuous everywhere.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 3, 'The deck''s origin is placed half a cell south-west of the field''s map origin. What does that buy?', '["It centres the field within the grid so the structure is symmetric about the middle of the model.", "A well whose map coordinates are multiples of the 100 m cell lands exactly on a cell centre rather than on a boundary.", "It removes the need for a coordinate transform between the map frame and the deck frame.", "It aligns the grid with the seismic bin grid, which is offset by half a bin from the map grid by convention in most survey designs."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A well on a cell boundary belongs to two cells and needs a tie-break, and tie-breaks differ between tools. Ekene-1 at (1000, 1000) sits at the centre of cell (11, 11) because of the offset. A transform is still required, and the offset is what makes it land conveniently.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 4, 'TOPS holds 900 values on this grid. What is each one?', '["The depth to the top of each cell, which is why there is one per column per layer.", "The average depth of the whole column, from which the layer interfaces are computed symmetrically.", "The depth to the top of the reservoir, which may be shallower than layer 1 where the model starts below the true top.", "The depth to the top of layer 1 in that column."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Deeper layers stack conformably beneath it, so the whole structure of the model is in those 900 numbers. It is the top of layer 1 of the MODEL, which is the top of the modelled interval and is only the top of the reservoir if the modeller included everything.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 5, 'TOPS values are listed in Eclipse natural order, with I varying fastest. What happens if that order is transposed?', '["The structure is transposed, and every summary statistic of the surface is unchanged, so nothing about the numbers looks wrong.", "The deck fails to parse, because the value count no longer matches the declared grid dimensions and the parser reports the mismatch.", "The depths are assigned to the wrong layers, so the model becomes vertically inverted.", "The simulator detects the inconsistency against the well positions and reports a geometry error."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A transposed surface has the same depth range, the same mean and the same histogram. What changes is where the crest is, and therefore where the oil is. The count is unchanged so it parses, and no simulator checks a surface against well positions.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 6, 'The Ekene layer thicknesses sum to 34.585155812896204 ft and the second layer is the thickest. Where did the proportions come from?', '["A uniform division of the net pay into five equal layers, adjusted for the structure''s dip.", "The core description, which logged five beds at those thicknesses in the type well.", "The waterflood course''s Dykstra-Parsons layer column, rescaled from its nominal 84 ft to the mapped net pay.", "The seismic resolution limit, which sets the thinnest layer the model can justify at about six feet on this survey."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The proportions 18, 22, 16, 14 and 14 carry over from the waterflood analysis and the absolute scale comes from the geoscience booking. The layers are deliberately not equal, and the most permeable is the second rather than the first, which is a waterflood lesson the deck inherits.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 7, 'This emitter has no NTG keyword. How does the Ekene deck carry net-to-gross?', '["In the porosity: 0.2 is the gross porosity already multiplied by the net-to-gross of 0.8.", "In the layer thicknesses: the dz values are already net pay, at a porosity of 0.2 for net rock.", "In the permeability, which is reduced by the net-to-gross so that the flow capacity through the layer is correct.", "It does not carry it at all, which is why the deck''s pore volume exceeds the booking''s."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The alternative would be a gross thickness of about 43 ft with an NTG applied separately, and both give the same pore volume. They give different depths to every interface below layer 1, and different answers to anything depending on gross thickness. Nothing about the numbers reveals which convention is in use, so a deck must state it.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 8, 'DZ for this grid is written as five tokens rather than 4500 numbers. Why five?', '["Because the emitter writes one value per layer and the simulator interpolates the rest.", "Because five is the maximum number of distinct values a compressed block may carry.", "Because the block is written once per layer and the simulator repeats it across the whole grid automatically.", "Because thickness is constant within a layer, so each layer needs one repeat count of 900."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each token is 900 copies of that layer''s thickness, and 900 is the cells per layer. That is the general pattern: a property constant within a layer compresses to one token per layer, and the blocks that do NOT compress are the ones carrying real spatial variation.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 9, 'Which block in the Ekene GRID section does not compress, and what does that tell you?', '["TOPS, because every column has its own depth, and the blocks that resist compression are the ones carrying real spatial information.", "PERMX, because permeability varies between the five layers rather than being constant.", "DX, because the cell size in the I direction varies across the structure.", "PORO, because porosity was populated cell by cell from the property model."]'::jsonb, 0, 'PORO, PERMX, PERMY and PERMZ are all layer-constant here and compress to five tokens each; DX and DY are constant everywhere and compress to one. TOPS is 900 distinct values wrapped across lines, and it is most of the section''s length. That diagnostic works on any deck.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 10, 'Line wrapping inside a long block is described as meaningless. Meaningless to whom?', '["To both, since the format ignores whitespace entirely and a deck can be written on one line.", "To the reader, since a wrapped block is harder to follow than one value per line.", "To the simulator, which reads a block as a stream of tokens until its slash; it matters only to the person reading it.", "To neither, since the wrap width is declared in RUNSPEC and changing it changes how the block is parsed and valued."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A deck can be reformatted to half the lines and be byte-different, semantically identical and equally correct. That is why counting lines tells you less about a deck than counting values, and there is no declared wrap width.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 11, 'A GRID block fails to parse. What should you count before looking at anything else?', '["The number of values the block should contain: nx times ny times nz for a cell property, or nx times ny for TOPS.", "The number of lines in the block, against the wrap width declared for the deck.", "The number of slashes, since each record needs one and the block needs a closing one.", "The number of distinct values, since a compressed block may not carry more than one distinct value per layer and a longer list is rejected."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A repeat count that is one short leaves the block a value short, the parser reads the next keyword as the missing value, and the error appears somewhere else entirely. Counting the values catches it directly. The symptom is the same as a missing slash, which is why the count is the discriminating check.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 12, 'Ekene''s 4500 cells are described as a teaching-scale model. What is the main cost of refining a grid?', '["Run time, which grows faster than linearly with cell count and dominates every other consideration.", "Data: every cell needs a porosity and a permeability, and refining does not add control points.", "Memory, which grows with cell count times the number of report steps and fills a disk.", "Numerical stability, since finer cells force the solver to take shorter timesteps to remain convergent."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Refining a 100 m grid to 25 m gives sixteen times the cells populated from the same six wells, so the extra cells are interpolation and interpolation at higher resolution adds no information. Run time and memory are real and secondary; output volume rather than memory is what fills disks.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 13, 'What does a coarse grid do to a displacement front?', '["It sharpens it, because a coarse cell cannot represent the transition zone behind the front.", "It leaves it unchanged, because the front position is computed from the fractional flow rather than from the grid.", "It delays it, because a coarse cell must fill completely before water passes to the next one.", "It spreads it over roughly one cell as it moves, predicting a smoother and earlier breakthrough."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That is numerical dispersion, and it is an error rather than a physical effect. It is the strongest argument for refinement in a waterflood study, and it is why a coarse model can be trusted for volumes long before it can be trusted for timing.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 14, 'What justifies refining a grid?', '["A forecast that disagrees with the history match, since refinement is the standard first response.", "A run time short enough to afford it, since more cells are better whenever they are affordable.", "Data at the finer scale, a feature smaller than a cell that matters, or a demonstrated sensitivity that stops when you refine further.", "A reviewer request, since grid resolution is the most commonly challenged assumption in a study."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The last of the three is a grid sensitivity study and it is the only real test: if halving the cell size changes the forecast materially the coarse answer was resolution-limited, and if it does not, refining further buys run time. More cells reduce discretisation error and say nothing about whether the properties in them are right.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 15, 'Five layers over 34.6 ft is about 7 ft per layer. What does that make invisible?', '["Nothing, since a layer''s properties are averages and an average carries the thin bed''s contribution.", "Anything thinner than about 7 ft, such as a 2 ft shale, which can only be represented through its effect on vertical permeability.", "The permeability contrast between layers, which needs at least ten layers to resolve.", "The contact, which cannot be placed within a layer and must fall on an interface."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A grid is a resolution, and a resolution is a statement about what you have decided not to model. A thin shale can be smeared into a layer''s vertical permeability, which represents its EFFECT without representing it. The contact does fall within layers, and the cell-centre rule is how the simulator handles that.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-tables', 1, 'The PROPS section carries DENSITY, PVTO, PVDG, PVTW and ROCK. What do all five have in common?', '["Each is a table indexed by pressure, so the simulator interpolates all five the same way between rows.", "Each hands the simulator numbers rather than a correlation, so the choice of correlation stays with whoever wrote the deck.", "Each is optional, because a simulator will fall back on internal correlations for any block that is absent from the deck entirely.", "Each describes a single phase, so a three-phase model needs exactly three of them and the other two are for output only."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A deck contains no correlations at all. PVTW and ROCK are lines of constants rather than tables, none of them is optional for a live-oil model, and DENSITY covers all three phases at once. What they share is that they are values, and the provenance of those values lives outside the file.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-tables', 2, 'Ekene''s ROCK entry reads a reference pressure of 3200 psia and a compressibility of 4e-6 per psi. Why does the reference pressure matter?', '["Because the rock compressibility is only valid above the bubble point, and 3200 psia is where the reservoir starts.", "Because the simulator uses it as the datum for equilibration, which is why it matches the EQUIL datum pressure exactly.", "Because pore volume is reported relative to that pressure in the output, so changing it rescales every reported volume in the run.", "Because a compressibility describes a change FROM somewhere, so it is meaningless without the pressure it is referenced to."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The same applies to PVTW, which also quotes 3200 psia. Compressibility is a relative change per unit pressure and the pore volume it is relative to has to be pinned somewhere. The equilibration datum is a separate keyword in SOLUTION, and rock compressibility has no bubble-point dependence.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-tables', 3, 'Why does oil need a table where water needs one line?', '["Because oil carries dissolved gas that comes out below the bubble point, shrinking the oil and raising its viscosity in a way no single compressibility describes.", "Because oil is more compressible than water by two orders of magnitude at reservoir conditions.", "Because the oil formation volume factor varies with temperature and water''s does not.", "Because oil viscosity is rate-dependent and must be tabulated against shear as well as pressure."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Formation water has effectively no dissolved gas, so its properties change slowly and smoothly with pressure and a compressibility captures them. Above the bubble point oil is also nearly linear, which is why PVTO''s undersaturated branch is short. Neither fluid''s viscosity is rate-dependent in this model.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-tables', 4, 'PVTO is described as two-dimensional in a one-dimensional format. What are the two dimensions?', '["Pressure and temperature, since the table is generated at a single reservoir temperature and interpolated elsewhere.", "Formation volume factor and viscosity, which are the two properties each record supplies against pressure.", "How much gas is dissolved, and what pressure the oil is at.", "Saturation pressure and bubble point pressure, which coincide only at the top record of the table."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Those are independent because oil can sit ABOVE its bubble point, holding all the gas it has while being compressed further. So the table is a set of records, one per solution gas ratio, each giving the saturated point and optionally continuing with undersaturated pressures at the same ratio.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-tables', 5, 'Ekene''s PVTO saturated nodes run 0.2, 0.3 and 0.4 Mscf/stb at 1000, 1500 and 2000 psia. What is the significance of the last one?', '["2000 psia is the initial reservoir pressure, so the last node describes the fluid as it was at discovery.", "2000 psia is the bubble point, which is why the highest solution gas ratio sits there and why that record carries the undersaturated branch.", "2000 psia is the lowest pressure the run will reach, so nodes below it exist only to satisfy the format.", "2000 psia is the datum pressure, so the last node is the one the equilibration uses to place the contacts."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The initial pressure is 3200 psia and the field never falls below the bubble point, so the saturated nodes below pb exist to make this a valid live-oil table rather than because the reservoir will visit them. The datum pressure is a SOLUTION-section quantity.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-tables', 6, 'Along Ekene''s undersaturated branch, Bo falls from 1.21728 at 2000 psia to 1.2 at 3200 psia while viscosity rises. What does that reversal signal?', '["That the bubble point has been crossed: below it rising pressure means more dissolved gas and a larger Bo, above it pressure simply compresses the liquid.", "That the table has been entered in the wrong order, since Bo should increase monotonically with pressure throughout.", "That the oil is being modelled as slightly compressible above the bubble point, which is an approximation the deck could have avoided by carrying more undersaturated nodes.", "That the viscosity correlation and the volume correlation disagree above the bubble point, which is normal and tolerated."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The maximum of Bo sits exactly at the bubble point, and a table that misses that turning point has its bubble point in the wrong place. The row at 3200 psia gives exactly 1.2, which is the initial formation volume factor the volumetric booking used, so the deck and the booking agree at the initial condition by construction.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-tables', 7, 'SWOF''s first row is Sw = 0.35 with krw = 0 and krow = 0.9. What are those three numbers?', '["The residual water saturation, the water endpoint mobility, and the oil endpoint at residual oil.", "The initial water saturation of the model, the water mobility at that saturation, and the oil mobility measured at the contact.", "The connate water saturation, the fact that connate water is completely immobile, and the oil''s maximum relative permeability.", "The irreducible water saturation from the capillary curve, which is 0.25 in the SCAL fit, rounded up for the table."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Connate water fills 35 percent of the pore space and none of it can move, which is what krw of exactly zero says. The oil is at its maximum mobility because no water is competing with it. The SCAL course''s capillary irreducible saturation of 0.25 is a different quantity, deliberately below the kr table''s connate value.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-tables', 8, 'SWOF closes at Sw = 1 and SGOF at Sg = 0.65. Why the difference?', '["SWOF is the primary table and must span the full range, while SGOF is secondary and only needs the mobile range.", "Gas saturation is measured relative to the hydrocarbon pore volume rather than the total, so its maximum is scaled.", "The gas table stops at the maximum gas saturation observed in the laboratory experiment, which happened to be 0.65.", "Connate water never leaves, so gas can reach at most one minus the connate water saturation, while a cell in the water leg genuinely sits at Sw of one."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A table running to Sg of 1 describes saturations the rock cannot reach and, worse, puts the gas endpoint mobility at the wrong saturation. Both saturations in this format are fractions of the total pore volume, and 0.65 follows from the connate water rather than from an experiment.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-tables', 9, 'What is the five-second check that catches a wrongly closed saturation table?', '["Add SWOF''s first saturation to SGOF''s last: if the two tables came from the same rock, connate water plus maximum gas must be exactly one.", "Compare the two tables'' row counts, which must match because both are evaluated on the same saturation grid.", "Confirm both tables end at a relative permeability of zero for the oil phase, which is the residual oil condition.", "Check that the capillary pressure columns of both tables agree at the contact depth, which is the one place where the two saturation systems have to meet."]'::jsonb, 0, 'For Ekene, 0.35 plus 0.65 is one. When that sum is not one, one of the two tables has the wrong axis and you have found it without reading a single mobility value. Row counts differ legitimately at 22 and 21, and both tables do end with the oil immobile, which is true whatever the axis.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-tables', 10, 'Ekene''s SWOF and SGOF both carry a capillary pressure column of zeros. What does that assert?', '["That the rock is water-wet, since a water-wet system has no capillary pressure in the oil-water table.", "That the transition zone is not being modelled, so contacts are sharp and saturation depends only on which side of the contact a cell sits.", "That capillary pressure is carried in EQUIL instead, which is where the contacts are defined.", "That the SCAL course did not measure it, so the deck leaves the column at its default of zero rather than inventing laboratory values it does not have."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The SCAL course did build a real capillary curve from a Leverett J-function and used it to show the crest is drier than the flanks, and none of that is in the deck. For a field with 49 m of relief and a transition zone of a few metres that is defensible; on a low-permeability field with a transition zone taller than the oil column it would be badly wrong.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-tables', 11, 'Between two rows of a table, what does a simulator do?', '["Cubic spline interpolation, which preserves the curvature of the underlying model between the tabulated points.", "It selects the nearer row, so the table is a step function and the row spacing sets the resolution directly.", "Linear interpolation, so the table is a polyline through your points rather than the curve you meant.", "It refits the underlying analytic model from the rows and evaluates that, which is why the exponents can be recovered."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The curve you meant exists only at the rows. For a convex function linear interpolation OVERESTIMATES between nodes, which is the same effect the SCAL course found when it resampled J-curves through a log-linear evaluator. The analytic model is gone once the table is written.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-tables', 12, 'Outside a table''s range, what does a simulator use?', '["A linear extrapolation of the last two rows, which is why tables should extend well beyond the expected range.", "The nearest physically valid value, computed from the endpoint saturations rather than from the table.", "It refuses to continue and reports that the run has left the table''s range, which is why the range must be checked first.", "The end value, held flat, with no extrapolation."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That makes the ends of a table a silent trap: a pressure below the lowest PVT row gets the lowest row''s properties, so the properties stop changing at exactly the pressure where they would be changing fastest. The check is to confirm the run''s range sits inside the table''s, and almost nobody does it.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-tables', 13, 'Ekene''s PVTO saturated branch was designed to be linear in pressure. What follows about interpolating between its three nodes?', '["The interpolation is exact for this fixture, which is a property of the designed fluid rather than of oil, so you should not expect exactness elsewhere.", "The interpolation is exact for any live oil, because the saturated formation volume factor is linear in pressure by definition for every live oil.", "The interpolation is inexact, because three nodes cannot represent a curve regardless of its shape.", "The interpolation is irrelevant, because the reservoir never visits pressures between the nodes."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Real saturated Bo curves bend, and a three-node table on a real fluid would carry genuine interpolation error. The Material Balance course made the same point about its formation volume factor track: the interpolation was exact because the underlying line was straight, and knowing why is what stops you expecting it elsewhere.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-tables', 14, 'Ekene''s densities are 54.02790214067278, 64.30084 and 0.0572715 lb/ft3 for oil, water and gas. What conditions are those at?', '["Reservoir conditions at the datum depth, which is why they are quoted alongside the equilibration pressure.", "Surface conditions, which the simulator combines with the formation volume factors to get reservoir densities.", "Standard conditions of 60 degrees Fahrenheit and one atmosphere, which differ from surface conditions by a small thermal correction.", "Bubble point conditions, since that is where the three phases coexist and a single density set is unambiguous."]'::jsonb, 1, 'They follow from three gravities: oil at 32 API, brine at a specific gravity of 1.03 and gas at 0.75 relative to air. Because the simulator derives reservoir densities from them, a mistake in DENSITY does not only affect buoyancy, it tilts the whole initial pressure field through the equilibration.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-tables', 15, 'Gas volumes in this deck are in thousands of standard cubic feet. What is the practical consequence for the solution gas ratio?', '["It appears as 400000, since the conversion multiplies rather than divides the familiar field value.", "It appears as 400 either way, because solution gas is dimensionless and unaffected by the gas volume unit.", "It appears as 0.4 rather than 400, and a factor-of-a-thousand slip produces no parse failure and no absurd-looking table.", "It appears as 0.4 only in PVTO, while RSVD carries the same quantity in scf per stock tank barrel for readability at depth."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That single factor is the most common PVT error in a deck. It produces a reservoir whose oil carries a thousandth of the gas it actually carries, and nothing about the file looks wrong. RSVD carries the same unit as PVTO, and the ratio has units of gas volume per liquid volume rather than being dimensionless.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-initialisation', 1, 'EQUIL takes a datum depth and a pressure at that datum. What is a datum?', '["The top of the reservoir, which is where the pressure is conventionally reported for a field-wide comparison.", "A reference depth, and nothing more: it is simply the depth at which you happen to be quoting a pressure.", "The depth of the oil-water contact, since that is the one depth both fluid systems share in common.", "The mid-point of the hydrocarbon column, chosen so the quoted pressure is representative of the whole accumulation."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Any depth would do, provided the pressure quoted is the pressure at THAT depth. The pair is what matters: a pressure without its datum is not a pressure, and a deck that changes datum without changing pressure has silently repressurised the field. Conventions vary between companies and none is more correct.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-initialisation', 2, 'Ekene''s datum sits about 12 ft deeper than its oil-water contact. Why is that not a mistake?', '["The datum was deliberately placed in the water leg so the equilibration would use the water gradient throughout.", "The datum must always lie below the contact so that the pressure integration proceeds upward through both phases.", "The datum is the mean of all 900 column tops, and more than half of them sit below the contact in the water leg, so their average lands below it.", "The contact was mapped in metres and the datum computed in feet, so the two are offset by the conversion."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A datum in the water leg is perfectly legal and the pressure quoted there is the water-phase pressure at that depth. It is only surprising if you expected the datum to mean something physical, which it does not. The integration proceeds in both directions from the datum whatever its position.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-initialisation', 3, 'What does a simulator actually do with the EQUIL line?', '["It integrates each phase''s density up and down from the known pressure at the datum to get a pressure everywhere, then assigns each cell a fluid from which side of the contact it sits on.", "It solves the flow equations to steady state and takes the result as the initial condition for the run.", "It reads the initial saturations from the SOLUTION section directly, cell by cell, and computes pressures from them.", "It places the contacts and leaves the pressures to be computed at the first timestep once the wells are open."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The result is a static, gravity-consistent state with no flow anywhere, which is what equilibration means: the model starts at rest. There is no per-cell saturation array in this deck, and nothing is deferred to the first timestep.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-initialisation', 4, 'Ekene''s contact is at 5118.110236220472 ft. Where did that number come from?', '["The lowest known oil in the six wells, taken as the shallowest possible contact for a conservative booking.", "The free water level derived from the SCAL course''s capillary curve, which sits below the mapped contact.", "The depth at which the pressure gradients of oil and water intersect, computed from the two densities.", "The geoscience courses'' mapped oil-water contact at 1560 m, converted to feet."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every volumetric number the field has ever booked rests on it. The SCAL course''s free water level is a different and deeper depth, separated from the contact by the capillary entry height, and this deck sets the capillary pressure at the contact to zero, which makes the two coincide in the model.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-initialisation', 5, 'In a block-centred model, how is a cell straddling the contact treated?', '["Partially, with a saturation proportional to the fraction of the cell above the contact.", "Entirely oil or entirely water depending on which side its CENTRE falls, so the modelled contact is a staircase rather than a plane.", "As a transition cell, with saturations interpolated from the capillary pressure curve across the whole of the cell''s own vertical thickness at that column.", "It is refined automatically into sub-cells so the contact can be represented exactly where it falls."]'::jsonb, 1, 'There is no partial cell. The staircase''s tread is a cell and its riser is a layer thickness, and the roughness that introduces is exactly what the Professional tier''s volumetric reconciliation measures. Automatic refinement is not something a simulator does to a Cartesian grid.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-initialisation', 6, 'Ekene has no gas cap. How does the deck say so?', '["By placing a gas-oil contact above the shallowest column top, where it cannot bite, so the whole box stays in the oil leg.", "By omitting the gas-oil contact from EQUIL entirely, which the simulator reads as a declaration that there is no free gas anywhere.", "By setting the gas saturation to zero in every cell through an explicit array in the SOLUTION section.", "By declaring only two phases in RUNSPEC, so the model has no gas phase to place a contact for."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That is the idiomatic way: not by omitting the contact but by putting it somewhere it cannot apply. The model does carry a gas phase, because near-wellbore pressures fall below the bubble point even when field pressure does not, so a two-phase declaration would leave the deck unable to represent its own producers'' drawdown.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-initialisation', 7, 'Both of Ekene''s EQUIL capillary pressures are zero. What does that make the contact?', '["An approximation to the free water level, offset by the entry pressure of the rock as measured in the laboratory.", "Irrelevant, since with zero capillary pressure the saturations are decided by the relative permeability endpoints instead.", "The free water level: the depth where the phase pressures are equal is also the depth where water saturation reaches one.", "A transition zone of zero thickness centred on the mapped contact, above and below which saturation varies smoothly."]'::jsonb, 2, 'With a non-zero entry pressure those two depths separate, and the SCAL course made a lesson of exactly that separation. Setting it to zero here is consistent with the zero capillary columns in SWOF and SGOF; a deck carrying a Pc curve in PROPS and zero in EQUIL would describe two different rocks.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-initialisation', 8, 'RSVD in Ekene''s deck gives 0.4 Mscf/stb at both the shallowest top and the deepest base. What does that assert?', '["That the oil is saturated at the base and undersaturated at the crest, which is the usual gradient in a thick column.", "That the solution gas was measured at two depths and interpolated linearly between them, as the keyword requires.", "That the reservoir has a compositional gradient too small to resolve, so the two ends are reported as equal within tolerance.", "That solution gas is constant with depth, so every barrel of oil starts with the same dissolved gas wherever it sits."]'::jsonb, 3, 'In a thick column deeper oil can hold more gas and a real gradient would carry different values at top and bottom. Ekene has about 62 ft of oil column and the Material Balance course modelled it as a single tank with one solution gas ratio, so a constant is consistent with the analysis the deck came from.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-initialisation', 9, 'The RSVD value of 0.4 Mscf/stb is not arbitrary. What must it match?', '["The top node of the PVTO table, which is the solution gas ratio at the bubble point.", "The average solution gas produced over the history, which is where the ledger''s ratio of 400 scf/stb comes from.", "The gas gravity of 0.75, converted to a solution gas ratio through the correlation used for the gas table.", "The initial gas in place divided by the initial oil in place, which is how the volumetric booking defines it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'If RSVD said 0.5 and PVTO topped out at 0.4, the deck would initialise oil with more gas than any row of its own table describes and the simulator would extrapolate a table it should not. The check is one line: the RSVD value must appear in PVTO, and for an undersaturated reservoir it should be the highest ratio the table carries.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-initialisation', 10, 'Ekene starts at 3200 psia with a bubble point of 2000 psia. What does that make every cell at time zero?', '["Saturated, since the solution gas of 400 scf/stb is the maximum the oil can hold at reservoir temperature.", "Undersaturated by 1200 psi, so no free gas exists anywhere in the model at the start of the run.", "At the bubble point exactly, which is why the PVTO table''s top node sits at 2000 psia rather than at 3200.", "Undersaturated by 1200 psi in the crest and saturated at the base, following the pressure gradient down the column."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is a property of the initial STATE rather than of the fluid. The same oil in a reservoir at 1800 psia would start saturated, with free gas, and the deck would need a gas-oil contact and a gas cap. The gradient over a 62 ft column is far too small to take the base to the bubble point.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-initialisation', 11, 'Nothing in the deck states the oil in place. Where does that number come from?', '["It is read from the volumetric booking through a comment block that the simulator parses for reporting purposes.", "It is computed after the first timestep, once the well controls have established the drainage volume belonging to each of the seven wells.", "It is a consequence of the grid, the porosity, the contact and the PVT table, computed by the simulator during initialisation and printed.", "It is supplied in RUNSPEC as a check value, and the simulator warns if its own calculation disagrees."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That printed number is the first thing to read in any run output, because every recovery factor the study quotes is a fraction of it. Comments are ignored entirely by a simulator, and nothing about the initialisation depends on the wells.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-initialisation', 12, 'A mistake in the DENSITY block affects more than buoyancy. What else?', '["The initial pressure field, because the equilibration integrates each phase''s density away from the datum to get pressure everywhere.", "The relative permeability endpoints, which are scaled by the density ratio between the phases.", "The formation volume factors, since the PVT tables are internally normalised to the surface densities.", "The well indices, because the density enters the near-wellbore flow calculation alongside the viscosity."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A cell 60 ft above the datum sits at a lower pressure by the oil gradient over 60 ft, and that gradient comes from the surface densities combined with the formation volume factors. So a density error tilts the whole initial pressure field, which changes every well''s drawdown from the first timestep.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-initialisation', 13, 'What does equilibration assert about the state of the reservoir at time zero?', '["That the wells have been shut in long enough for pressures to build up to a static value.", "That the reservoir is at its bubble point throughout, which is the only state in which all phases coexist stably.", "That the aquifer is in pressure communication with the oil leg, which is what allows a single datum to describe both.", "That nothing at all is moving: every phase pressure gradient is exactly balanced by gravity."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That is a modelling assumption rather than an observation. A real reservoir at discovery may have regional flow, tilted contacts or an active aquifer sweeping across it, and a deck that equilibrates has assumed all of those away.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-initialisation', 14, 'You are handed a new deck. What is the standard first check on its initialisation?', '["Compare the reported oil in place against the volumetric booking, which is the only test of the contact depth.", "Run it with no wells for a short period and confirm nothing changes; a pressure drift means the initialisation and the property fields disagree.", "Confirm the datum depth lies within the grid''s depth range, since a datum outside it cannot be integrated from.", "Check that the equilibration converged, which the simulator reports as an iteration count in the initialisation section of its own printed log file."]'::jsonb, 1, 'If the initial state is not in equilibrium the model moves at the first timestep with no wells open, fluids redistribute, and the drift looks like a physical response. The usual culprits are a density inconsistent with the PVT tables or a contact placed outside the grid. Comparing against the booking is also worth doing and tests something different.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-initialisation', 15, 'Equilibration is nine lines against the GRID section''s 137. Why does it decide more than its length suggests?', '["Because it runs before the grid is read, so an error in it invalidates the geometry that follows.", "Because it is the only section a simulator cannot recompute if it is wrong, so an error there is unrecoverable once the run has started.", "Because it fixes how much oil the model contains, and every recovery factor the study ever quotes is a fraction of that.", "Because it sets the timestep the solver begins with, which propagates through the whole run."]'::jsonb, 2, 'An error in the initial oil in place does not wash out with time; it scales every result the study produces. The wells act ON the initial state and they act on it forever, because the material balance of the whole run starts from the volume equilibration decided. SOLUTION is read after GRID and PROPS, not before.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wells-and-schedule', 1, 'A well enters a deck through WELSPECS and COMPDAT. What does each answer?', '["WELSPECS declares the well''s completion interval and COMPDAT supplies the perforation density and skin for each interval within it.", "WELSPECS declares that the well exists and where its head is, together with the depth its pressure is reported at; COMPDAT says which cells it is open to.", "WELSPECS carries the surface location and COMPDAT the bottom-hole location, so together they define the trajectory of the wellbore.", "WELSPECS names the well and COMPDAT names the group it belongs to, which is how facility constraints and field-level targets reach individual wells through the schedule."]'::jsonb, 1, 'They are split because a well is drilled once and re-completed many times, so the declaration and the completions change on different timescales. That split also means a well can exist with no open connections, which is what a shut-in well is. The group is part of WELSPECS.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wells-and-schedule', 2, 'Ekene''s vertical wells carry k1 = 1 and k2 = 5. How many connections is that per well?', '["Four, because the range from the first to the fifth layer spans four intervals between the layer interfaces of the column.", "Ten, because each layer contributes an inflow and an outflow connection to the wellbore at its upper and lower faces.", "One, because a vertical well through a single column is modelled as a single connection whose length is the whole net pay.", "Five, one per layer."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The range is inclusive, so k1 of 1 and k2 of 5 is five connections. Six vertical wells at five connections each is thirty COMPDAT records, plus the side-track''s own list. Off-by-one here is the commonest completion error and it silently removes a layer from the well.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wells-and-schedule', 3, 'What is a connection, physically, in a simulator?', '["A term coupling one cell to the well''s pressure, whose strength is a well index computed from the cell''s permeability and size, the wellbore radius and the direction.", "A short length of wellbore, modelled explicitly with its own pressure drop and friction between adjacent connections.", "A perforation tunnel, whose length and density set the flow area available between the reservoir rock and the wellbore.", "A boundary condition applied at the cell face nearest the wellbore, with the flow rate imposed and the pressure computed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A simulator does not model a wellbore at all in this formulation. That is why the direction flag and the wellbore radius appear in COMPDAT rather than in WELSPECS: both are properties of how this well meets THIS cell, and the well index is where they enter.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wells-and-schedule', 4, 'Ekene''s producers run on ORAT at 1500 stb/d with a bottom-hole pressure floor of 1200 psia. What happens when the reservoir can no longer deliver the target?', '["The well is shut in automatically and reported as inactive until the reservoir pressure recovers above the floor.", "The simulator raises a warning and continues at the target rate, allowing the bottom-hole pressure to fall below the stated floor for as long as the target is held.", "The well switches to producing whatever the pressure floor allows, which is normal and is the most important behaviour in the block.", "The timestep is cut until the well can meet its target, which is why a well approaching its limit slows the whole run down."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A well on rate control is on rate control until it is not, and over a field''s life most wells spend most of their time on the limit rather than on the target. A study reporting that a well made its target without saying whether it was ever pressure-limited has left out the interesting part.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wells-and-schedule', 5, 'Why is an injector''s pressure limit a maximum where a producer''s is a minimum?', '["Because injectors are controlled on reservoir volume rather than surface volume, which inverts the sense of the constraint.", "Because injection pushes pressure up, and the ceiling stands in for the formation parting pressure above which the rock fractures.", "Because the injector''s limit is measured at the wellhead and the producer''s at the sandface, so the two are quoted in opposite senses.", "Because an injector cannot fall below reservoir pressure by definition, so a minimum would never bind and the format uses a maximum instead."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Above the parting pressure the water opens fractures instead of entering the matrix, and the flood changes character entirely. A deck whose injection ceiling sits above parting pressure will happily fracture the field and not tell you. Both limits are bottom-hole quantities.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wells-and-schedule', 6, 'A real WCONPROD record carries several limits at once. What does the well honour?', '["The most restrictive of them at every timestep, which means a well can be limited by something the author did not intend to set.", "The first limit listed, with the others held in reserve and applied only if the first is relaxed later in the schedule.", "The oil rate limit, since the control mode names it and the remaining limits are advisory rather than binding.", "The average of the binding limits, which is how a simulator avoids the numerical discontinuity of switching abruptly between one limit and another."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A default water rate limit left in place can shut a well in mid-run, and the reported cause will be the limit rather than the physics. That is why a well that stops unexpectedly is worth checking against every limit in its record rather than against the reservoir.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wells-and-schedule', 7, 'DATES advances the run to a calendar date and TSTEP by a list of lengths. Which does Ekene''s history use, and why?', '["TSTEP, because a history of uniform monthly reporting is simpler to build and the difference between month lengths is negligible.", "Both alternately, so that the calendar is honoured at year ends while the intervening months use a uniform mean-month step.", "DATES, because TSTEP is reserved for prediction periods and a simulator will refuse it before the history has completed.", "DATES, because the ledger records calendar months of differing length and a uniform step would apply each month''s rates over the wrong interval and misallocate volume."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A schedule advancing by a uniform 30.4375 days cannot express a 28-day February followed by a 31-day March, so rates would be applied over the wrong intervals and cumulative volumes would drift. The prediction tail does use TSTEP, correctly, because nothing in a forecast is tied to a calendar.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wells-and-schedule', 8, 'The prediction tail uses 60 steps of 30.4375 days. What is that number?', '["The average length of the 36 ledger periods in the history, so the two halves of the schedule use a consistent interval.", "The interval at which the solver writes a restart file, which is fixed by the format rather than chosen by the author.", "A mean month: 365.25 divided by 12.", "The maximum timestep the solver will take before cutting, which is why a longer value would slow the run rather than speed it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'It is a mean month including the leap-year quarter, and it is a fixed default rather than anything derived from Ekene. Using it means the prediction steps do not line up with calendar months, which is fine for a forecast and would be wrong for a history.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wells-and-schedule', 9, 'A DATES or TSTEP entry defines a report step. What is the relationship to the steps the solver takes?', '["They are the same thing, which is why refining the report interval reduces the time-discretisation error of the solution.", "None directly: the solver chooses its own step size, cutting it when convergence is hard, and may take many internal steps inside one report step.", "The solver takes exactly ten internal steps per report step by default, and that ratio is configurable in RUNSPEC.", "The report step is the maximum the solver may take, so a shorter report interval forces shorter solver steps everywhere and therefore a more accurate answer."]'::jsonb, 1, 'So the 36 entries in Ekene''s history are 36 points at which results are written rather than 36 solves. Report steps cost output volume and nothing else; solver steps cost run time, and setting a tiny report interval buys a much larger output file and the same answer.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wells-and-schedule', 10, 'In a SUMMARY vector name, what do the first letter and a trailing H mean?', '["The first letter is the scope, with F for field and W for well, and a trailing H means the observed value rather than the simulated one.", "The first letter is the phase, with F for fluid and W for water, and a trailing H means the value is reported hourly.", "The first letter is the frequency of reporting and the trailing H marks a vector that is written only at the end of the history period rather than at every report step.", "The first letter distinguishes field from well and the trailing H marks a high-resolution vector written at every solver step."]'::jsonb, 0, 'So FOPR is field oil production rate, WBHP is well bottom-hole pressure, and FOPRH is the observed field oil rate. The H vectors are not simulator output at all: they are the numbers you put in the schedule, echoed back so a history match can be plotted from one file.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wells-and-schedule', 11, 'Adding a vector to the SUMMARY section changes what?', '["The run time, because each additional vector must be accumulated by the solver at every internal timestep it takes.", "The report step interval, because the simulator writes more frequently when more vectors have been requested of it.", "The solution, slightly, because requesting a well vector forces the simulator to compute that well''s terms more precisely.", "The output file, and nothing else."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Two runs of the same deck differing only in SUMMARY produce identical reservoir behaviour. That is worth being certain about, because it means vectors can be added freely when re-running without worrying that the comparison has been perturbed.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wells-and-schedule', 12, 'What can SUMMARY not give you?', '["Anything at well level, since well quantities are written to a separate file that the SUMMARY section does not control.", "Anything before the first report step, because the initial state is written only to the restart file rather than to the summary series at all.", "Anything the model does not itself compute, such as sweep efficiency, the swept volume, or how much of the injection went out of zone.", "Anything about injection, since injected volumes are inputs rather than outputs and are recorded in the schedule instead."]'::jsonb, 2, 'There is no vector for swept volume and none for out-of-zone injection. Those are quantities you derive from the vectors, which is what the waterflood course spent three tiers doing. Well vectors, initial values and injection rates are all available.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wells-and-schedule', 13, 'Ekene''s deviated side-track is called EK6-ST rather than EKENE-6ST. Why?', '["EK6-ST is the operator''s own naming convention for side-tracks, which the deck follows so results can be joined to the production database.", "A well name is at most eight characters in the Eclipse format, and EKENE-6ST is nine, so the emitter refuses it outright.", "A name containing more than one hyphen is rejected by the parser, so the longer form would have failed to load in any case.", "Side-track names must differ from their parent in the first three characters so the two are not merged during results aggregation."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Truncation is worse than a parse error, because two wells whose names truncate to the same eight characters become one well: a simulator seeing a second declaration for an existing name treats it as a redefinition, and one well silently disappears.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wells-and-schedule', 14, 'Well names are described as effectively permanent once a study has any history. Why?', '["Because every per-well output vector is keyed by name, so the name in the deck is the name in the results and in every plot and comparison built from them.", "Because a simulator caches the name-to-index mapping in the restart file, so a rename invalidates every restart the study has saved.", "Because the eight-character limit means most short names are already taken, so renaming usually forces a scheme change across the field.", "Because well names are validated against the field''s licence records, which cannot be changed once a study has been submitted."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A rename mid-study breaks the join between a run and its predecessors, so it is a data migration rather than a cosmetic edit. The eight-character limit means the tidying is best done before the first run, and studies that defer it usually live with whatever the first author typed.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wells-and-schedule', 15, 'Each Ekene well carries its own bottom-hole reference depth, taken as its column''s mid-depth. What follows?', '["Every well reports its pressure at the same datum, so the reported values can be compared directly across the field.", "A well''s reference depth moves when its completion changes, so a re-completion shifts its reported pressure history.", "Wells on different parts of the structure report bottom-hole pressures that are not directly comparable without accounting for the depth difference.", "The reference depth must lie inside the completed interval, which is why a well completed in only one layer cannot use the mid-depth of its whole column as its reference."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A simulator reports and controls bottom-hole pressure AT that depth, so two wells with identical downhole conditions and different reference depths report different pressures. Because the depth is stated per well rather than derived, changing a completion does not change it, which is usually what you want and is occasionally a surprise.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 1, 'Capstone field 1 asks for the deck''s cell count. What is the likely mistake?', '["Counting only the cells above the contact, which is the number that carries oil and is the one a volumetric report would quote.", "Multiplying by the number of report steps, which is how the output file''s size is computed and is easy to confuse with the model size.", "Counting columns rather than cells, which gives 900, the number of values TOPS carries and a plausible-looking answer in its own right.", "Counting the connections rather than the cells, since a fully completed vertical well touches exactly one cell per layer and the two counts are therefore closely related."]'::jsonb, 2, 'TOPS has one value per column and PORO one per cell, so the two counts differ by a factor of the layer count. That relationship is the check: if your two numbers do not differ by exactly five on this grid, you have read one of them wrong.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 2, 'Capstone field 2 asks for the crest depth. What is the check that it is right?', '["It must be shallower than the contact at 5118.110236220472 ft, because otherwise the structure holds no oil anywhere and the field does not exist.", "It must be shallower than the mean of the well tops, because the crest is by definition above the average of the control points used to make the surface.", "It must equal the shallowest of the six mapped well tops, because kriging is an exact interpolator and cannot go above its data.", "It must be deeper than the shallowest layer base, since TOPS records the top of layer one and the crest is measured to the base of it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two mistakes are common: reading the shallowest layer BASE rather than the shallowest top, which gives a deeper number, and converting to metres, which gives about a third of the right answer on a field everyone discusses in metres. A kriged surface can rise above its data between control points.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 3, 'Capstone field 3 asks for the first layer''s thickness. What would assuming equal layers give?', '["The correct answer for layer three only, since the five thicknesses are symmetric about the middle layer of the column.", "A value too large by the ratio of the second layer''s thickness to the first, since layer two is the thickest of the five.", "The correct total but the wrong distribution, so the sum across the five layers would still check out and only the individual layer values would be wrong.", "One fifth of the net pay, which is wrong for every one of the five layers because the column''s proportions are 18, 22, 16, 14 and 14."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The thicknesses follow the waterflood course''s proportions of 18, 22, 16, 14 and 14, so layer 1 takes 18 of 84. The check is that your five thicknesses must sum to the stated net pay and the second must be the largest. Assuming equal layers does preserve the total, which is why the sum check alone would not catch it.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 4, 'A learner submits 0 as the saturation at which the water-oil table begins. Which table have they actually read?', '["Giving the residual oil saturation of 0.25, which is the other endpoint of the mobile range and is quoted alongside it in the SCAL fit.", "Giving the first saturation of SGOF instead, which is zero.", "Giving the capillary irreducible saturation of 0.25 from the SCAL course, which sits deliberately below the kr table''s connate value.", "Giving the water saturation used in the volumetric booking, which is the same 0.35 but arrived at from a different measurement entirely."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Read the table''s name before its rows. The check is that this value is the connate water saturation, so it must lie strictly between zero and one and must equal one minus the last SGOF saturation. The booking''s 0.35 is in fact the same number, which is why that option is not the mistake it looks like.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 5, 'Capstone field 5 asks where SGOF closes. What is the classic wrong answer?', '["0.75, which is one minus the residual oil and is where the water-oil table''s oil mobility reaches zero.", "0.6, which is the maximum gas saturation the laboratory experiment reached before the sample was depressurised.", "1, which is the correct answer for SWOF and is the classic axis-closure error, because it describes gas saturations this rock can never reach.", "0.35, which is the connate water saturation and is where the companion water-oil table begins its own range."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A gas-oil table closes at one minus connate water, because connate water never leaves the rock. The check is that field 4 plus field 5 must equal exactly one; if they do not, one of the two tables has the wrong axis and you have found it without reading a mobility value.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 6, 'A vertical well is completed with k1 = 1 and k2 = 5, and a learner submits 4 connections. What have they done?', '["Off by one: k1 of 1 and k2 of 5 is five connections, not four.", "Counting the side-track''s connections instead, which is a different number because its completions came from a trajectory rather than from a layer range.", "Counting the connections across all six vertical wells, which is the total number of COMPDAT records rather than the count for one well.", "Counting the layers the well passes through rather than the layers it is open to, which differ whenever a completion is selective."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The range is inclusive. The check is that the total COMPDAT count for the six vertical wells should be six times this number. The other options are all real ways to get a different number and none of them is the off-by-one the field is testing for.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 7, 'What do all six Associate capstone fields have in common?', '["Each requires a conversion between the deck''s field units and the metric units the field was originally mapped and reported in.", "Each is read directly from a block of the deck rather than computed, and each likely mistake is reading the right kind of number off the wrong block.", "Each is a quantity the simulator computes during initialisation and reports in its log before the first timestep is taken.", "Each depends on the contact depth, which is why the capstone states the contact explicitly in its prompt rather than leaving the reader to look it up."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is why the advice before submitting is to name the keyword each answer was read from. Half the near-misses in this tier come from that sentence not being written. Only the crest depth involves a conversion, and none of the six comes from a simulator log.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 8, 'The Associate tier''s summary describes the deck''s rock in one table. Which layer is the most permeable?', '["The first, since a layer-cake model conventionally orders layers from best to worst reservoir quality downward.", "The third, at 250 md, which is the median permeability and the value the SCAL course used to scale its J-function.", "The fifth, at the base of the column, because the coarsest sand is deposited first in a shallowing-upward sequence.", "The second, not the first."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Layer 2 carries 607.7507038307907 md against layer 1''s 173.81198701129736. That ordering is deliberate and it is a waterflood lesson the deck inherits: the fast layer is not the top layer, so a reader who assumes the flood proceeds down the column will be wrong about which layer breaks through first.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 9, 'What did this tier deliberately not ask about the deck?', '["Where any of it came from: the tops are 900 numbers derived from six measurements and this tier took them as given.", "Whether the deck runs, since running it is outside the whole course rather than outside this tier in particular.", "What the simulator does with it, since the solver''s behaviour is a separate discipline from the format the deck is written in.", "How large the model is, since cell count is a Professional concern that depends on the data available to populate it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The oil is one of several defensible descriptions of the same fluid, and the layer column is one analysis of a sand. Those are the Professional tier''s questions, and they are the difference between reading a deck and trusting one. Running it is indeed outside the course, but that is not what this tier set aside.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 10, 'The Professional tier is motivated by one fact about the deck''s structure. What is it?', '["The deck''s structure was calibrated against the volumetric booking, so its far-field shape carries no independent information at all.", "The deck''s contact was mapped rather than logged, so every column''s assignment to oil or water rests on an interpretation.", "The deck contains 900 column depths and exactly six of them were measured, so the ratio of derived to measured data is 150 to 1.", "The deck''s structure is a Cartesian box with no faults, so any compartmentalisation in the real field is absent from the model entirely."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ratio of derived to measured is 150 to 1, and that is the normal condition of every reservoir model ever built. The calibration and the absence of faults are both real and both are things the Professional and Expert tiers take up rather than the fact that motivates the move.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 11, 'What kind of reading does the Professional tier require that this tier did not?', '["Reading the deck in the order the simulator reads it, section by section, rather than jumping to the block a question is about.", "Holding the deck against something outside it: the mapping that made the surface, the volumetric booking, the laboratory report and the production database.", "Reading the results file alongside the deck, since a number''s provenance can only be established from what the model produced with it.", "Reading the simulator''s source, since the treatment of defaults and warnings differs between implementations in ways a deck cannot express."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A deck read on its own is always self-consistent, because it is one file. A deck read against its sources is where the disagreements live, and finding them is the whole content of the next tier.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 12, 'The tier says to take the units seriously going forward. Which seams does the next tier cross repeatedly?', '["Psia and psig, Fahrenheit and Rankine, and darcies against millidarcies, all of which appear in the PROPS section.", "Surface and reservoir conditions for gas only, since both of the liquid phases are quoted at stock tank conditions consistently throughout the whole deck.", "Calendar months and mean months, which is the only unit distinction that changes a number rather than merely its label.", "Metres against feet, stock tank against reservoir barrels, and standard cubic feet against thousands of them, several times per lesson."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every one of those three is a place a real study has gone wrong. The month-length distinction is real and belongs to the schedule rather than to units, and pressure and temperature scales do not vary within this deck.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 13, 'The tier''s closing advice includes knowing which section a number lives in. Why does that matter for the next tier?', '["Because every question there is about a specific block and the answer usually involves comparing it against a block in another section.", "Because the Professional capstone is timed, so a reader who has to search for a keyword will not finish within the allowed period.", "Because the sections are read in order by the simulator, so a number''s meaning depends on which sections have already been parsed.", "Because the Professional tier edits the deck rather than reading it, and an edit in the wrong section produces a different deck entirely."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A reader who has to search for PVTO will lose the thread of a comparison that spans PROPS, SOLUTION and the volumetric booking. Editing the deck is the Expert tier''s work rather than the Professional tier''s.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 14, 'Two thirds of the Ekene deck is its SCHEDULE section. What does that proportion say about the study?', '["It has an unusually detailed grid description, since the schedule includes the completions for every well in the model.", "It was generated rather than written by hand, since a hand-written deck would balance the sections more evenly.", "It carries a long production history, one rate block and one date per month, which is what a history-matched study looks like from the outside.", "It has been left largely at defaults elsewhere, which is why the few sections that do carry explicit data end up appearing disproportionately large by comparison."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A deck that is nearly all GRID is a big static model with a short run; one with a tiny PROPS section is probably using defaults you should go and check. Ekene''s GRID section is short precisely because its properties are constant and compress, and its PROPS section is a full set of tables.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 15, 'The tier''s exercise asks you to estimate how many cells lie more than 500 m from the nearest well. What is that estimate for?', '["To identify the cells whose properties should be excluded from the volumetric calculation as insufficiently constrained by data.", "To make concrete how much of the model is interpolation rather than measurement, and where the regional mean rather than the data decides the answer.", "To determine the kriging range, which should be set to the distance at which half the cells fall outside the influence of any well.", "To decide where infill wells would most improve the model, which is the standard use of a distance-to-control map in field development."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The point is proportion. Six measurements support 900 depths, and the cells far from every well are where the regional mean rather than the data is deciding the answer. No cell is excluded from the volumetrics, and the kriging range is a modelling choice rather than something derived from a distance count.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'A deck validates and a simulator runs it without complaint. What has that established?', '["That the deck describes a physically consistent reservoir, since a simulator refuses states it cannot equilibrate.", "That the deck''s units are internally consistent, because the parser checks each block against the unit system declared in RUNSPEC.", "That the grammar parsed and the required blocks were present.", "That the deck''s wells lie inside the grid and its tables span the range the run will visit, which are the two checks a simulator performs on load."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Acceptance is a statement about the file, and no validator has ever seen the field. A deck can describe a different reservoir, in the wrong units, with a fluid from elsewhere, and still be perfectly well formed. Unit consistency in particular is not checkable, because a number carries no unit of its own.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'Which describes the relationship between a deck and a model?', '["The deck describes a reservoir and the model is what the simulator builds from it, so the deck is upstream of every pressure and saturation in the run.", "The model is the deck plus the results, so a study''s model is only complete once a run has finished.", "The deck is the static half and the model is the dynamic half, which is why wells and schedule live outside the deck.", "They are the same object in two formats, so a model can be written back out as a deck without loss."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The pressure field, the saturations and the mass balances all live in the model the simulator constructs. Wells and the schedule are part of the deck, results are downstream of both, and a model cannot be inverted back into the deck that produced it.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'Every number in the Ekene deck traces to one of the four earlier courses in the reservoir module. Which supplied the layer column?', '["Material Balance, which produced the tank model the layer thicknesses were derived from by matching its pressure history.", "SCAL and Displacement, which fitted the Corey curves whose endpoints set the movable oil in each of the five layers.", "Decline Curve Analysis, whose per-well Arps fits established the relative deliverability of the layers the wells are completed in.", "Waterflood Management."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The layer thicknesses and permeabilities are the waterflood course''s Dykstra-Parsons column. Material Balance supplied the oil, SCAL the rock curves, and the waterflood course also supplied the production history. The decline course''s fits do not appear in the deck at all.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'Why must the six deck sections appear in a fixed order?', '["Because the sections are stored as separate records and the file''s index assumes them in that sequence.", "Because a simulator reads a deck in a single pass, so each section depends on the ones before it having been read already.", "Because reordering them changes which defaults apply, since a default is taken from the most recently read section.", "Because the format was standardised before random access was practical, so the ordering is historical rather than functional."]'::jsonb, 1, 'RUNSPEC declares dimensions so GRID can be checked, GRID establishes cells so PROPS has something to apply to, PROPS defines fluids so SOLUTION can equilibrate, and SOLUTION sets the state SCHEDULE starts from. Nothing goes back to reinterpret an earlier section.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'What does the SUMMARY section decide?', '["What is recorded, and therefore what you can see afterwards; it changes nothing about the physics.", "Which cells the solver reports on, which changes the internal timestep and therefore the solution slightly.", "The report step interval, so a longer vector list produces finer output in time.", "Which quantities the solver computes, so omitting a vector saves the work of computing it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two runs differing only in SUMMARY produce identical reservoir behaviour. The practical consequence is that a missing vector is a deck edit and a re-run rather than a physics problem, and it is the cheapest kind of mistake to fix.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'A depth of 5055.774278215223 is read from the Ekene GRID section. What unit is it in and how do you know?', '["Metres, because depths are conventionally metric regardless of the declared unit system for other quantities.", "Feet, because the TOPS keyword carries its own unit declaration immediately before the values begin.", "Feet, because RUNSPEC declared the deck to be in FIELD units and nothing restates the units afterwards.", "It is ambiguous, which is why a deck should state units per block rather than once in RUNSPEC."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ekene was mapped in metres and its contact at 1560 m appears in the deck as 5118.110236220472 ft. A reader assuming metric would conclude the field is over three times deeper than it is, and nothing in the file would contradict them.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'This course does not run a deck. What does it teach instead, and why is that the boundary?', '["How to read results files, since the deck itself is generated automatically in modern workflows.", "How to build a deck a simulator will accept and an engineer can defend, because that is a distinct skill and it decides whether the solver''s answer means anything.", "How to configure a simulator, since the deck is only one of several inputs a run requires.", "How to history match, since that is the step between a deck and a forecast and it is where judgement enters."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Petrolord''s Reservoir Simulation Studio is where a Petrolord deck goes to be run. The Expert tier does spend a module reading results and grades none of it, because the engines this course rests on emit decks and do geometry rather than solving flow equations.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'The Ekene grid is 30 by 30 by 5. How many values must TOPS contain?', '["4500, one per cell, because every cell needs its own top depth in a block-centred grid.", "150, one per layer per row, because TOPS is written layer by layer in the same order as DZ.", "901, because the interfaces between layers number one more than the layers themselves.", "900."]'::jsonb, 3, 'TOPS holds one depth per COLUMN, giving the top of layer 1, and deeper layers stack conformably beneath it. That is why TOPS and a cell property such as PORO differ in length by a factor of the layer count, and counting them is the first check when a GRID block fails to parse.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'The deck''s origin is half a cell south-west of the field''s map origin. What does that achieve?', '["It removes the need to transform coordinates between the map frame and the deck frame at all.", "It centres the accumulation in the grid so the structure is symmetric about the model''s middle.", "A well at map coordinates that are multiples of 100 m lands on a cell centre rather than on a boundary.", "It aligns the model with the seismic bin grid, which is conventionally offset by half a bin from the map grid."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A well on a boundary belongs to two cells and needs a tie-break, and tie-breaks differ between tools. Ekene-1 at (1000, 1000) sits at the centre of cell (11, 11) because of the offset, while Ekene-2 at a northing of 1150 m does not, which the Professional tier takes up.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'TOPS values are listed with I varying fastest. What is the symptom of transposing that order?', '["The deck fails to parse, because the value count no longer matches the grid dimensions declared in RUNSPEC.", "The structure is transposed while the depth range, the mean and the histogram are all unchanged, so nothing about the numbers looks wrong.", "The layers become inverted, so the model''s deepest layer is reported as its shallowest.", "The simulator detects the mismatch against the wells and reports a geometry error before initialising."]'::jsonb, 1, 'What changes is where the crest is, and therefore where the oil is. The value count is unchanged so it parses, and no simulator checks a surface against well positions. Only posting the wells on the model''s own map catches it.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'The Ekene deck has no NTG keyword. How is net-to-gross carried?', '["In the layer thicknesses, which are already net pay, combined with a porosity of 0.2 that is the porosity of net rock.", "In the porosity, which is the gross value already multiplied by the net-to-gross of 0.8.", "In the permeability, which is scaled down so that the layer''s flow capacity comes out correct in spite of the gross thickness used.", "It is not carried at all, which is the reason the deck''s pore volume exceeds the volumetric booking''s."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The alternative is a gross thickness of about 43 ft with an NTG applied separately, and both give the same pore volume. They give different depths to every interface below layer 1, so a deck must state which convention it uses because nothing about the numbers reveals it.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'DX for this grid is one token: 4500*328.0839895013123. What does the star do?', '["It multiplies the two, giving the total east-west extent of the grid in feet.", "It marks the value as applying to all cells not explicitly assigned elsewhere in the block.", "It separates the cell count from the cell size, which are two independent fields of the same record.", "It repeats the value that follows it 4500 times, once for every cell in the grid, which is why the block is a single token."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Reading it as multiplication gives a cell size of about 1.5 million feet, which is absurd enough to catch, and the same mistake on a smaller repeat count is not absurd at all. A wrong repeat count leaves a block short and the parser consumes the next keyword, so the error is reported after the real one.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'Which block in Ekene''s GRID section does not compress, and what does that tell you?', '["PERMX, because permeability differs between the five layers rather than being constant across the grid.", "PORO, because porosity was populated cell by cell from a geostatistical property model.", "TOPS, because every column has its own depth, and the blocks that resist compression are the ones carrying real spatial variation.", "DZ, because layer thickness varies with the structure and therefore differs from column to column."]'::jsonb, 2, 'PORO, PERMX, PERMY and PERMZ are all layer-constant and compress to five tokens each. TOPS is 900 distinct values and is most of the section''s length. That diagnostic works on any deck: the incompressible blocks are where the modelling effort went.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'What is the main cost of refining a grid?', '["Run time, which grows faster than linearly with cell count and dominates every other consideration.", "Data: every cell needs a porosity and a permeability, and refining the grid does not add any new control points to supply them.", "Numerical stability, since finer cells force shorter timesteps for the solver to remain convergent.", "Output volume, which grows with cell count times report steps and is what fills a disk on a long run."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Refining a 100 m grid to 25 m gives sixteen times the cells populated from the same six wells, so the extra cells are interpolation and interpolation at higher resolution adds no information. Run time and output are real and secondary, and the solver''s adaptive stepping is not destabilised by finer cells.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'What do DENSITY, PVTO, PVDG, PVTW and ROCK have in common?', '["Each hands the simulator numbers rather than a correlation, so the choice of correlation stays with whoever wrote the deck.", "Each is indexed by pressure, so the simulator interpolates all five identically between their rows.", "Each is optional, since a simulator falls back on internal correlations for any block absent from the deck.", "Each describes a single phase, so a three-phase model needs exactly three of them and the remaining two exist only for reporting."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A deck contains no correlations. PVTW and ROCK are lines of constants rather than tables, none is optional for a live-oil model, and DENSITY covers all three phases at once. What they share is that they are values, and their provenance lives outside the file.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'Why does PVTO have records rather than rows?', '["Because each record describes one laboratory sample, so a field with several samples carries several records.", "Because a record groups the properties of one phase, and PVTO covers oil and its dissolved gas together.", "Because live oil has two independent variables, the dissolved gas and the pressure, and a record per solution gas ratio is how a one-dimensional format carries them.", "Because records may be reordered freely while rows may not, which matters when a table is assembled from several sources."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Oil can sit above its bubble point, holding all the gas it has while being compressed further, so pressure and solution gas are independent. Each record gives the saturated point for its ratio and may continue with undersaturated pressures at the same ratio.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'Along Ekene''s undersaturated branch Bo falls as pressure rises. What does that reversal mark?', '["A tabulation error, since the formation volume factor of a live oil increases monotonically with pressure.", "The bubble point: below it more pressure means more dissolved gas and a larger Bo, above it pressure simply compresses the liquid.", "The transition from the correlated part of the table to the designed part, which were built by different methods.", "The point at which the oil becomes denser than the water, which is why the undersaturated branch is only ever carried on the topmost record of the table."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The maximum of Bo sits exactly at the bubble point, and a table that misses that turning point has its bubble point in the wrong place. The row at 3200 psia gives exactly 1.2, which is the factor the volumetric booking used, so the deck and the booking agree at the initial condition.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'SWOF''s first row is Sw = 0.35 with krw = 0. What does the zero say?', '["That the water phase has not yet been initialised at that saturation, so no mobility has been assigned to it.", "That the table begins below the irreducible saturation, where water exists but the measurement could not resolve its mobility.", "That capillary forces hold the water in place until the threshold pressure is exceeded, which the Pc column would carry.", "That connate water is completely immobile."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Connate water fills 35 percent of the pore space and none of it can move, which is what a relative permeability of exactly zero states. The SCAL course''s capillary irreducible saturation of 0.25 is a different quantity, deliberately below the kr table''s connate value.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'What is the five-second check on a pair of saturation tables?', '["Add SWOF''s first saturation to SGOF''s last saturation: if both tables describe one rock, the two must sum to exactly one.", "Confirm both tables have the same number of rows, since both are evaluated on the same saturation grid.", "Confirm both end with the oil relative permeability at zero, which is the residual oil condition in each of the two systems and is common to both tables.", "Confirm the capillary pressure columns agree at the contact depth, which is where the two systems meet."]'::jsonb, 0, 'For Ekene, 0.35 plus 0.65 is one. When the sum is not one, one of the two tables has the wrong axis and you have found it without reading a mobility value. Row counts differ legitimately at 22 and 21, and both tables do end with the oil immobile whatever the axis.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'Between two rows of a table, what does a simulator do, and which way does the error run?', '["Cubic interpolation, which preserves curvature and has no systematic direction of error.", "Nearest-row selection, so the table is a step function and the error alternates in sign between rows.", "Linear interpolation, which systematically overestimates between the nodes whenever the underlying curve is convex.", "It refits the underlying model from the rows, so there is no interpolation error at all once the fit has converged on the original parameters."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The curve you meant exists only at the rows, and a chord across a convex function sits above it. That is the same effect the SCAL course found resampling J-curves through a log-linear evaluator. The analytic model is gone once the table is written and cannot be recovered by the simulator.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'A pressure falls beyond the last row of a PVT table. What value does the simulator use?', '["A linear extrapolation of the last two rows, which is why tables should extend beyond the expected range.", "The end value, held flat, with no extrapolation, which is why a run''s range must be checked against the table''s.", "The nearest physically valid value, recomputed from the endpoint saturations rather than being read from the table itself at all.", "It stops and reports that the run has left the table, which is the only safe behaviour for an extrapolation."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A pressure below the lowest PVT row gets that row''s properties, so the properties stop changing at exactly the pressure where they would be changing fastest. The check is one line of arithmetic that almost nobody does.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'What is an equilibration datum?', '["A reference depth and nothing more: it is simply the depth at which you happen to be quoting the pressure that accompanies it.", "The top of the reservoir, which is where pressure is conventionally reported so that different fields can be compared on a common basis.", "The depth of the oil-water contact, since that is the one depth both fluid systems have in common.", "The mid-point of the hydrocarbon column, chosen so the quoted pressure represents the whole accumulation."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Any depth would do provided the pressure quoted is the pressure at THAT depth. The pair is what matters, and a deck that changes datum without changing pressure has silently repressurised the field. Conventions vary between companies and none is more correct.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'Ekene''s datum sits below its oil-water contact. Why?', '["It was placed deliberately in the water leg so the equilibration would use the water gradient throughout the model.", "The contact was mapped in metres and converted, while the datum was computed in feet from the surface, so the two carry different rounding errors.", "It is the mean of all 900 column tops, and more than half of them lie in the water leg, so their average falls below the contact.", "A datum must lie below the contact so that pressure integration proceeds upward through both phases in sequence."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A datum in the water leg is perfectly legal and the pressure quoted there is the water-phase pressure at that depth. It surprises only if you expected the datum to mean something physical, which it does not.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'How is a cell straddling the contact treated?', '["Partially, with saturation proportional to the fraction of the cell lying above the contact depth.", "Entirely oil or entirely water depending on which side its centre falls, so the modelled contact is a staircase.", "As a transition cell, with saturations interpolated from the capillary pressure curve across the whole of its own thickness.", "It is subdivided automatically so the contact can be represented at the depth where it actually falls."]'::jsonb, 1, 'There is no partial cell. The staircase''s tread is a cell and its riser is a layer thickness, and that roughness is exactly what the Professional tier''s volumetric reconciliation measures against the booking.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'Ekene has no gas cap. How does the deck express that?', '["By omitting the contact from EQUIL, which a simulator reads as a positive declaration that there is no free gas anywhere in the whole model.", "By declaring only oil and water in RUNSPEC, so the model carries no gas phase to place a contact for.", "By setting the initial gas saturation to zero through an explicit array in the SOLUTION section of the deck.", "By placing a gas-oil contact a hundred feet above the shallowest column top, where it lies outside the model and cannot bite."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A contact outside the model is a contact that never applies. The model does carry a gas phase, because near-wellbore pressures fall below the bubble point even when field pressure does not, so a two-phase declaration would leave the deck unable to represent its own producers'' drawdown.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'RSVD gives 0.4 Mscf/stb at both ends of the Ekene model. What must that value match?', '["The top node of the PVTO table, which is the solution gas ratio the oil carries at its bubble point of 2000 psia.", "The average solution gas of the produced stream over the history, which the ledger records as 400 scf/stb.", "The gas gravity of 0.75, converted through the correlation that generated the gas table.", "The initial gas in place over the initial oil in place, which is how the volumetric booking defines the solution gas ratio for the accumulation as a whole."]'::jsonb, 0, 'If RSVD exceeded the table''s top node, the deck would initialise oil with more gas than any row of its own table describes and the simulator would extrapolate a table it should not. The check is one line: the RSVD value must appear in PVTO.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'Nothing in the deck states the oil in place. Where does the number come from?', '["It is read from a comment block that the simulator parses for reporting, which is why decks carry the booking in a header.", "It is computed after the first timestep, once the well controls have established each well''s drainage volume.", "The simulator computes it during initialisation from the grid, the porosity, the contact and the PVT table, and prints it.", "It is supplied in RUNSPEC as a check value, and the simulator warns if its own calculation disagrees with it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That printed number is the first thing to read in any run output, because every recovery factor the study quotes is a fraction of it. Comments are ignored entirely, and nothing about initialisation depends on the wells.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'What is the standard first check on a new deck''s initialisation?', '["Compare the reported oil in place against the booking, which is the only test of whether the contact is right.", "Run it with no wells for a short period and confirm nothing changes; a pressure drift means the initialisation and the property fields disagree.", "Confirm the datum lies within the grid''s depth range, since a datum outside it cannot be integrated from.", "Check the equilibration iteration count in the log, which rises sharply when the initial state is inconsistent."]'::jsonb, 1, 'If the initial state is not in equilibrium the model moves with no wells open, and the drift looks like a physical response. The usual culprits are a density inconsistent with the PVT tables or a contact outside the grid. Comparing against the booking is also worth doing and tests something different.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'Why are WELSPECS and COMPDAT separate keywords?', '["Because WELSPECS is read in RUNSPEC and COMPDAT in SCHEDULE, so they belong to different sections of the deck.", "Because a well may belong to several groups, and the completion list has to be repeated once for each of them.", "Because the completion list can exceed the record length that a single keyword''s fields are allowed to occupy.", "Because a well is drilled once and re-completed many times over its life, so the declaration and the completions change on quite different timescales."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Splitting them lets a deck open and shut intervals through the run without redeclaring the well, and it means a well can exist with no open connections, which is what a shut-in well is. Both keywords live in SCHEDULE.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'A connection is described as a coupling rather than a piece of pipe. What sets its strength?', '["A well index computed from the cell''s permeability and dimensions, the wellbore radius and the direction the well crosses the cell.", "The perforation density and shot phasing, which are carried in the two fields following the wellbore radius.", "The pressure difference between the cell and the wellbore, recomputed at every timestep from the current state of the cell and of the wellbore above it.", "The length of wellbore in the cell alone, which is why a longer connection always delivers proportionally more."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A simulator does not model a wellbore in this formulation, which is why the radius and the direction sit in COMPDAT rather than WELSPECS: both describe how this well meets THIS cell. Length matters and is one term among several.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'Ekene''s producers are on ORAT at 1500 stb/d with a floor of 1200 psia. What is the most important behaviour in that block?', '["The target, since it is what the well produces for as long as the reservoir has oil to give.", "The floor, since it is a hard constraint the simulator will never allow the well to cross under any control mode whatsoever, whatever the target.", "The switch: a well on rate control is on rate control until it is not, and most wells spend most of their life on the limit.", "The mode, since ORAT fixes which phase the limits apply to and the others are then unconstrained."]'::jsonb, 2, 'If delivering 1500 stb/d would need a bottom-hole pressure below the floor, the well switches to producing whatever the floor allows. A study reporting that a well made its target without saying whether it was pressure-limited has left out the interesting part.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'Which does Ekene''s history use to advance time, and why?', '["TSTEP, because uniform monthly reporting is simpler and the difference between the individual month lengths is negligible when accumulated over three years.", "DATES, because the ledger records calendar months of differing length and a uniform step would apply each month''s rates over the wrong interval.", "Both alternately, so the calendar is honoured at year ends while intervening months use a mean-month step.", "DATES, because TSTEP may not appear before the history has completed and a simulator refuses a deck that uses it early."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A uniform 30.4375-day step cannot express a 28-day February followed by a 31-day March, so cumulative volumes drift. The prediction tail does use TSTEP, correctly, because nothing in a forecast is tied to a calendar.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'A DATES entry defines a report step. What is its relationship to the solver''s own steps?', '["None directly: the solver chooses its own step size adaptively and may take many internal steps inside one report step.", "They coincide, which is why refining the report interval reduces the time-discretisation error of the solution.", "The report step bounds the solver step, so a shorter report interval forces shorter solver steps everywhere and therefore more accuracy.", "The solver takes a fixed number of internal steps per report step, configurable through a RUNSPEC option."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene''s 36 history entries are 36 points at which results are written rather than 36 solves. Report steps cost output volume and nothing else; a tiny report interval buys a larger file and the same answer.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'Decode WOPRH.', '["Well oil production rate, hourly, which is the finest reporting frequency the format supports for a well vector.", "Well oil production rate at the historical datum, which differs from WOPR by the depth correction to the reference.", "Well oil production rate, observed.", "Weighted oil production rate, which averages across the wells of a group in proportion to their completions."]'::jsonb, 2, 'W is the well scope, OPR the quantity and the trailing H marks it as the observed value rather than the simulated one. The H vectors are not simulator output: they are the numbers put into the schedule, echoed back so a history match can be plotted from one file.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'Why is a well-name truncation collision worse than a parse error?', '["Because a truncated name cannot be joined back to the production database, so the results become unusable.", "Because the simulator continues with the first well''s completions and discards the second''s rates without reporting it.", "Because the eight-character limit applies to groups as well, so a collision propagates into the facility constraints.", "Because a parse error stops the run and is fixed in a minute, while a name collision silently merges two wells into one and reports nothing."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A simulator seeing a second WELSPECS for an existing name treats it as a redefinition rather than a new well, so one well disappears without any message. That is why every well name in a study should be checked for uniqueness in its first eight characters before the first run.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'The Ekene deck''s most permeable layer is which one?', '["The first, because a layer-cake model conventionally orders layers from best to worst quality downward through the column.", "The second.", "The third, at 250 md, which is the median and is also the permeability the SCAL course used to scale its J-function.", "The fifth, at the base, because the coarsest sand is deposited first in a shallowing-upward depositional sequence."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Layer 2 carries 607.7507038307907 md against layer 1''s 173.81198701129736. That ordering is deliberate and it is a waterflood lesson the deck inherits: a reader who assumes the flood proceeds down the column will be wrong about which layer breaks through first.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'What is the check that the crest depth is right?', '["It must be shallower than the contact at 5118.110236220472 ft, because otherwise the structure holds no oil anywhere and there is no field.", "It must equal the shallowest of the six mapped well tops, since a kriged surface cannot rise above its own data.", "It must be deeper than the shallowest layer base, since TOPS gives the top of layer one while the crest of the structure is conventionally measured to its base.", "It must fall within one layer thickness of the mean column top, which is what a gently dipping structure implies."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two mistakes are common: reading the shallowest layer BASE rather than the shallowest top, and converting to metres on a field everyone discusses in metres. A kriged surface can and does rise above its control points between them.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'The five layer thicknesses sum to the mapped net pay. What would assuming equal layers preserve, and what would it break?', '["It preserves the individual values of the middle layer and breaks the outer four, since the column is symmetric.", "It breaks the total as well, because the proportions 18, 22, 16, 14 and 14 do not divide the net pay into any whole number of equal parts.", "It preserves the total across the five layers and breaks every individual value, so a check on the sum alone would not catch it.", "It preserves both, since the model only uses the total thickness and distributes it evenly in any case."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The thicknesses follow proportions of 18, 22, 16, 14 and 14, so layer 1 takes 18 of 84. The check that does catch it is that the second layer must be the largest, which equal layers cannot reproduce.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'What did the Associate tier deliberately not ask?', '["Whether the deck runs, since that is outside the whole course rather than outside this tier alone.", "What the simulator does with the deck, since solver behaviour is a separate discipline from the file format.", "How large the model should be, since cell count depends on data availability rather than on the format.", "Where any of the deck''s numbers came from."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The tops are 900 numbers derived from six measurements and this tier took them as given, the oil is one of several defensible descriptions, and the layer column is one analysis of a sand. Those are the Professional tier''s questions and they are the difference between reading a deck and trusting one.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'What fact motivates the move to the Professional tier?', '["The deck''s structure was calibrated against the booking, so its far-field shape carries no independent information.", "The deck contains 900 column depths and six of them were measured, so the ratio of derived to measured data is 150 to 1.", "The deck''s contact was mapped rather than logged, so every single column''s assignment to oil or to water rests on an interpretation rather than a measurement.", "The deck''s grid has no faults, so any compartmentalisation in the real field is absent from the model entirely."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That ratio is the normal condition of every reservoir model ever built, and saying it out loud is the point. The calibration and the absence of faults are both real and are things the later tiers take up rather than the fact that motivates the move.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'What kind of reading does the Professional tier add?', '["Holding the deck against something outside it: the mapping, the booking, the laboratory report and the production database.", "Reading the deck strictly in the order a simulator reads it, rather than jumping to the block a question concerns.", "Reading the results alongside the deck, since a number''s provenance can only be established from what the model actually produced with it.", "Reading the simulator''s own documentation, since defaults and warnings differ between implementations."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A deck read on its own is always self-consistent because it is one file. A deck read against its sources is where the disagreements live, and finding them is the whole content of the next tier.'),
  ('sim', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'Two thirds of the Ekene deck is SCHEDULE. What does that say about the study?', '["It has an unusually detailed grid, since the completions for every well are written into the schedule.", "It was generated rather than hand-written, since a hand-written deck would balance its sections more evenly.", "It carries a long production history, which is what a history-matched study looks like from the outside.", "It is largely defaulted elsewhere, which is why the sections carrying explicit data look disproportionately large."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A deck that is nearly all GRID is a big static model with a short run; one with a tiny PROPS section is probably using defaults worth checking. Ekene''s GRID section is short because its properties are constant and compress, and its PROPS section is a full set of tables.');
